# Review and merge

`main` is protected by the ruleset in `.github/rulesets/main.json`:

- Changes reach `main` only through a squash-merged PR; no force-push, no deletion, no bypass.
- {% if stack_decided %}`checks` (the project's `just ci`), {% endif %}{% if release_workflow %}`image`, {% endif %}`private-refs`, `adr` and `gitleaks` must pass; the ruleset in
  `.github/rulesets/main.json` is the list. Never merge on red, even when the failure looks unrelated: fix it or park the PR.
- Reviews are not required, for anyone: the author merges. Code owners in `.github/CODEOWNERS` are requested as
  reviewers automatically, which is how a one-way change gets its review requested.
- The `doors` check classifies every PR from `.github/doors.toml`. A **one-way door** (reverting it after release
  may not restore an equivalent state: data reshaped or dropped, a contract others depend on, data that crossed a
  trust or tenancy boundary, an effect that cannot be called back, or a governance file) gets the `door:one-way`
  label and one comment saying why. The comment recommends a review and states that the ADR is required. Every other
  PR gets `door:two-way`, and a PR with neither label was not classified. The check never blocks. Work on a one-way
  change stops, and the PR does not merge, until its ADR is accepted by the named decider (`CONSTITUTION.md` §5).

Review requests go through GitHub (CODEOWNERS requests them automatically), not chat; the daily digest lists PRs
still waiting after {{ review_wait_hours }} h.

## Apply or change the ruleset

Edit `.github/rulesets/main.json`, merge it, then as a repo admin run `scripts/repo/apply-ruleset.sh`. The script
updates the ruleset named `main` or creates it.

## Tune the door check

Edit `.github/doors.toml`: migration globs and destructive patterns, declared contract and effect paths, regexes for
effectful calls (email, SMS, payment SDKs), and the OpenAPI spec. Set `external_consumers = false` and
`atomic_frontend_deploy = true` only when the API's sole consumer is this repo's frontend and both deploy together
with versioned assets; then breaking API changes count as two-way. Editing the file is itself one-way.

The classifier, its runner script and its config are read from the base branch, so a PR cannot loosen them for
itself; a PR that breaks the config fails the check. The workflow file runs from the PR, though: a PR that removes or
empties `.github/workflows/doors.yml` is not classified at all.

## Symptoms

| Symptom | Fix |
|---|---|
| No reviewer requested automatically | The owner in `CODEOWNERS` is not a user or team with write access; fix the entry |
| Required check "Expected — waiting for status" never starts | A job in `ci.yml` was renamed; update `required_status_checks` and re-apply |
| `doors` fails: "cannot read .github/doors.toml" | Fix the TOML syntax; the check only fails on its own config |
| A PR has no door label | The `doors` run failed or could not write labels (fork PR); read its log |
| A routine change is labelled one-way | Narrow the glob or pattern in `.github/doors.toml`; the label is advice, merge if you judge it two-way |
| `apply-ruleset.sh` returns 403 | Run it as a repo admin. Private repos on GitHub Free do not support rulesets |
